lessons.content:
Repeat. Refine. Repeat.
Why write the same instruction again and again?
            Learn how loops automate repetition and make your
            JavaScript programs smarter and more efficient.
Start → Check → Execute → Update → Repeat

Why do we need loops?
Imagine printing numbers from 1 to 100.
                    Writing 100 separate console.log() statements
                    would be slow and unnecessary. Loops solve this problem.

Repetition
Run the same instructions multiple times without rewriting code.

Automation
Let JavaScript handle repetitive tasks automatically.

Efficiency
Write shorter, cleaner and easier-to-maintain programs.

CODE:
console.log(1);
console.log(2);
console.log(3);
console.log(4);
console.log(5);


CODE:
for
(let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    console.log(i);
}
// Output: 1 2 3 4 5


How does a loop work?
Most loops follow a repeating process. A starting
                    value is created, a condition is checked, the code
                    runs, and the value is updated.

Initialize
Create the starting variable.

Condition
Check if the loop should continue.

Execute
Run the statements inside the loop.

Update
Change the variable and check again.

CODE:
for
(
    let i = 1;
// Initialize
i <= 5;
// Condition
i++
// Update
) {
    console.log(i);
// Execute
}


The for loop
A for loop is useful when you know how many times
                    you want to repeat a task.

CODE:
for
(let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    console.log("Welcome to JavaScript");
}
// Prints the message five times.


The while loop
A while loop repeats a block of code as long as
                    its condition remains true. It is useful when the
                    number of repetitions is not known in advance.

CODE:
let count = 1;
while
(count <= 5) {
    console.log(count);
    count++;
}
// Output: 1 2 3 4 5

Important: Remember to update the variable inside the loop.
            Otherwise, the condition may stay true forever and create
            an infinite loop.

The do...while loop
This loop executes its code block at least once,
                    then checks the condition. Even if the condition
                    is false initially, the code runs once.

CODE:
let number = 1;
do
{
    console.log(number);
    number++;
}
while
(number <= 5);
// Output: 1 2 3 4 5


do
Executes the code block first.

while
Checks the condition after execution.

Repeat
Runs again if the condition is true.

Compare the three loops
All three loops repeat code, but each has a
                    different use case.
LOOP
BEST USED WHEN
RUNS AT LEAST ONCE?
for
Repetition count is known
No
while
Condition controls repetition
No
do...while
Code must run before checking
Yes

Build your own loop
Choose a loop type, enter a starting number and
                    decide how many times it should repeat.
                    Run it and inspect the output.

Loop simulator

Control your loop
JavaScript gives you special statements to change
                    the normal flow of a loop.

break
Stops the entire loop immediately, even if the
                    condition is still true.

continue
Skips the current iteration and moves to the
                    next iteration.

CODE:
for
(let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
if
(i === 3) {
break
;
    }

    console.log(i);
}
// Output: 1 2


CODE:
for
(let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
if
(i === 3) {
continue
;
    }

    console.log(i);
}
// Output: 1 2 4 5


Nested loops
A nested loop is a loop placed inside another loop.
                The inner loop completes its repetitions for every
                single iteration of the outer loop.

CODE:
for
(let i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
for
(let j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
        console.log(i, j);
    }

}
// Output: (1,1) (1,2) (1,3)
//         (2,1) (2,2) (2,3)


